WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.840
It’s crazy when you think about all of
the different kinds of foods we eat.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.760
We just swallow and hope
it all works out for the best.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Well, as it turns out,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.680
there are better ways to think about keeping
our bodies humming healthfully along.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.080
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.800
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.400
Today we answer a wide
variety of your questions, like...

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.800
the importance of a
good breakfast smoothie,

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.680
what kind of scientific study
I’d like to see performed,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.600
and we start with an interesting
question from Keene

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.760
about whether there
have been any updates

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.120
on the therapeutic
use of psychedelics.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.760
So psychedelics was on my list of topics.&nbsp;
It's so fascinating, such an incredible area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.720
There's finally been some placebo-controlled&nbsp;
trials. You say, “Wait a second, how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.360
do a placebo-controlled trial?” Right? You kind of&nbsp;
notice when you're on mushrooms or whatever. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.480
no—how about we anesthetize people, make people&nbsp;
unconscious, then give them mushrooms or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.880
They have no idea when they wake up afterwards&nbsp;
whether they were on mushrooms or not. Does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.400
actually change the brain? Does it actually do&nbsp;
anything? Or do you actually need to have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.800
component? So many interesting questions. I&nbsp;
wanted to do a deep dive. There's the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:18.480
ketamine stuff. And I really wanted to cover it&nbsp;
all. Is MDMA going to hurt our brains? And just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.120
so many questions I just don't know the answers&nbsp;
to, and I was eager to find out. I get a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:33.280
questions about it. So I got about 300 meg worth&nbsp;
of PDFs, a couple hundred articles on ayahuasca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:42.800
LSD, mescaline, microdosing—talk about safety.&nbsp;
So I have the research. It's there. I just have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.800
to read it and write it. And unfortunately, it&nbsp;
all fell down before I was able to jump on it.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:59.440
Any data—this is actually something I looked into.&nbsp;
This is what do they call it? It's not a seaweed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:07.760
It actually grows on the shores in brackish water.&nbsp;
So it pulls up salt. So it's like this salty moss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:14.880
or something, this plant that has a high salt&nbsp;
content. And so it's a way to add salt to a food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:26.000
secretly. Pickleweed. Anyway, there was a whole&nbsp;
food plant-based muffin mix company that—like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.240
well, wait a second. How do you have&nbsp;
a whole food plant-based muffin? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.680
what can you use for sweetener? You use date&nbsp;
powder, right? Whole food. But what do you use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:43.840
for salt? Anyone who's baked knows you add salt&nbsp;
to baked goods to change the flavor profile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.840
If it's whole food plant-based, there can't&nbsp;
be any salt in it. And so what they used is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.640
this ingredient. And so I was like, “Oh, all&nbsp;
right. Well, let's look it up.” And so they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.480
effectively just adding salt. I forget—it's like&nbsp;
30% of the weight of this plant is pure salt. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:06.720
just sucks up the salt from the seawater. And so,&nbsp;
I mean, look at the sodium. It's off the charts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.800
as you'd expect, as if you just added&nbsp;
salt. So it's kind of a sneaky way.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.360
And so I contacted them. I was like, you can't do&nbsp;
this. You can't get away with this. And so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.800
actually changed it to potassium chloride, which&nbsp;
is the way to do it. You move from sodium salt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.960
potassium salt. So that's the way you can actually&nbsp;
cut down on sodium. So I would not eat this really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:34.720
salty plant, even though it's like a whole food,&nbsp;
right? But it's too much sodium. If you want your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:41.440
food salty, use potassium chloride, or season&nbsp;
it in a way where you don't need it so salty.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.600
Tyler says, “If I make a smoothie&nbsp;
with 80% of my vitamin mineral intake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:52.000
it will absorb the nutrients that I need.”&nbsp;
Well, I mean, it depends on your vitamin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.480
mineral status. Your body actually—if you're&nbsp;
low on calcium, your body absorbs more calcium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.080
If you're low on iron, your body absorbs more&nbsp;
iron. And vice versa: if you have too much iron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.920
your body actually doesn't want too much and so&nbsp;
puts the brakes on. I mean, if you had one meal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:17.920
a day that had 100% of your nutrient needs,&nbsp;
you would stay alive. I mean, you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.520
to split it up through the day. Your body would&nbsp;
probably, the first day, be like, whoa—one meal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:30.240
we got all this manganese or whatever. But it'll&nbsp;
eventually get to be like, oh, I guess that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:37.600
we're doing these days: one meal a day. And so I&nbsp;
gotta take my entire manganese allotment in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.000
one meal, and it'll do it. Our body's pretty&nbsp;
smart. So yeah, I wouldn't worry about it.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.120
In fact, I think for a lot of people, the&nbsp;
breakfast smoothie is probably the healthiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:53.120
meal of the day. That's one of the great things&nbsp;
about smoothies—you can add all sorts of noxious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.520
stuff. My smoothie this morning had so many&nbsp;
broccoli sprouts it was almost too much. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.960
been trying to experiment with adding more&nbsp;
and more broccoli sprouts, and I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.400
I don't taste them at all. It's amazing,&nbsp;
right? You mix it with the berries and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:14.800
just don't taste the broccolininess at all. Okay,&nbsp;
you do. Today was the first day I was like, “Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.960
my smoothie tastes like broccoli.” But it was a&nbsp;
lot of broccoli sprouts, right? I would not want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.440
to eat that many broccoli sprouts throughout the&nbsp;
day because it has a really kind of spicy flavor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:32.960
or radishy bite to it. So you make a smoothie,&nbsp;
you could add all sorts of greens and the amla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.040
and stuff that's just normally nasty. So&nbsp;
you could have just so much nutrition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:45.680
and then even if you falter later in the day, at&nbsp;
least you got a good solid, solid, solid base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:54.160
So I'm down with that strategy. Although&nbsp;
tomorrow I'm adding less broccoli sprouts.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:04.640
Let me see. HG. Oh, what am I eating today?&nbsp;
So this morning I had my sprout disaster. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.600
you make your own sprouts—broccoli sprouts.&nbsp;
Super easy to make. I think it's 25 cents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.840
per cup. Sprout them yourselves.&nbsp;
So on a nutrition-per-dollar basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.080
probably impossible to beat. I mean,&nbsp;
maybe a good sweet potato comes close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:27.920
but it's pretty amazing. So anyway, the list of&nbsp;
stuff I have in my smoothie would take all day.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:35.120
But for lunch, I had leftovers.&nbsp;
I knew I had 14 interviews today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:41.920
so I didn't have time to actually make food. And&nbsp;
so I had leftovers of a wonderful spaghetti sauce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:51.200
packed with veggies. Got some artichoke hearts&nbsp;
in there. And so I had that on some whole wheat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:59.760
spaghetti. And then for supper, I'm going to&nbsp;
do purple sweet potato fries. And then I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.520
know what else, but I'm excited for my purple&nbsp;
sweet potato fries. They're so hard to find in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:14.960
rural Virginia. Not all of us can live in big&nbsp;
fancy cities, but I finally found a source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.280
purple sweet potatoes. I haven't had them in a&nbsp;
while, so I'm super excited to make some fries.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:26.960
Anyway, all right. Next up, Silly Head—not one,&nbsp;
not two, but Silly Head Five—says, “If I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.800
see one study get performed, what would I want&nbsp;
to see put to the test?” I'm a big fan. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:40.960
so there are studies that would be really useful&nbsp;
and interesting scientifically, but ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:49.360
it's all about making people's lives better.&nbsp;
And so those are kind of two different things.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:55.920
In terms of having an actual impact on the&nbsp;
world, it would probably be a disease reversal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:02.160
study. I mean, that excites people. You do&nbsp;
some basic science thing which unveils some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.320
really interesting thing about some random&nbsp;
enzyme—maybe in 20 years that does something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.120
interesting to help people—but if you want&nbsp;
the kind of bang for your buck right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.400
I've been working on How Not to Hurt, this pain&nbsp;
management book through lifestyle. And there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:27.040
this series of amazing studies called Plants for&nbsp;
Joints on osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:32.560
randomizing people to a whole food plant-based&nbsp;
diet and seeing remarkable benefits. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.120
osteoarthritis is the leading cause of physical&nbsp;
disability among older men and women. The fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.960
that you can so improve pain and function just&nbsp;
by changing people's diets—that is so cool.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:49.920
And so those are the kind of studies&nbsp;
I would like to see, where people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.360
within a matter of weeks, change their&nbsp;
diet and not just their lab values change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:01.360
but their day-to-day function changes. They&nbsp;
can go up and downstairs without hurting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:08.720
They can play with their grandkids more. And it's&nbsp;
super common—not some rare little niche disease.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:16.160
So I'd love to see—how about back pain&nbsp;
studies? There haven't been any—oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:21.840
actually, I take that back. There haven't&nbsp;
been any plant-based dietary intervention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.240
studies for back pain, but I was about to say&nbsp;
there haven't been any randomized controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:31.200
dietary trials for back pain. There actually&nbsp;
were. I think there was a Mediterranean one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:38.640
Anyway, I'd like to see plant-based, since&nbsp;
blood flow through the vertebral arteries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.720
may indeed play a role in certain types of&nbsp;
low back pain. It would be nice to see if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:52.880
you actually improve that circulation, would you&nbsp;
actually see some benefit? That hasn't been done.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:59.760
So yeah—disease reversal studies, common chronic&nbsp;
disease reversal studies, that can excite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:06.480
imagination, grab headlines, and get people&nbsp;
thinking—particularly physicians thinking, wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:12.320
I have a hundred people in my practice with the&nbsp;
exact same condition. That's really frustrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:17.520
to treat. I can't make them better. Every time&nbsp;
they come to the office, I wish I could give them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:21.840
something. But what am I going to do—give them&nbsp;
another opioid prescription or something? No.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:27.520
Now all of a sudden there's a tool to actually&nbsp;
make people feel better. That's amazing.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:34.720
So that's the kind of study I would do.&nbsp;
And unfortunately, we're out of time.

